<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Code language="abide" caption="src/page.abide" code={`<script server>
import { GET } from '@abide/abide/server'

export const getGreeting = GET(() => \`Hello from Bun \${Bun.version}\`)
</script>

<script>
import { reactive } from '@abide/abide'

const greeting = reactive(() => getGreeting())
let count = reactive(0)
</script>

<h1>{greeting}</h1>
<button onclick={() => count += 1}>Clicked {count} times</button>`} />

    <p><em>1 file, 15 lines</em> — and the app serves <code>/</code>, with
    <code>getGreeting</code> answering at <code>/getGreeting</code> beside it.</p>

    <p>One file holds a function that runs on the server, the values, the markup that shows them,
    and the click that changes one. There is no API between the two halves, no store to declare,
    no component to register, and no re-render to ask for.</p>

    <Section title="Where the file goes decides the address">
        <p>The directory is the path, and a <code>page.abide</code> makes that directory a page.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Answers on</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/about/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/about</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/invoices/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/invoices</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/invoices/[id]/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/invoices/42</code>, with <code>42</code> readable as <code>route.params.id</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Every directory under <code>src</code> is a segment of an address, so
        <code>src/billing/page.abide</code> is <code>/billing</code>. Group an app by subject, and
        the addresses follow.</p>

        <p>A handler answers below its file's directory, under the name it was exported as:
        <code>getGreeting</code> in <code>src/page.abide</code> is
        <code>/getGreeting</code>, and the same export in
        <code>src/invoices/page.abide</code> would be
        <code>/invoices/getGreeting</code>.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/serve-the-app-under-a-sub-path')}>Sub-path mounting</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`<script server>` runs on the server, `<script>` sets up the page">
        <p><code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> runs on the server alone, once. What it exports is a
        handler — <code>GET</code> declares one — and the rest of the file calls it by the name it
        was declared with. The browser is never sent the block: the build generates a call under
        the same name in its place, so <code>Bun.version</code> is read where Bun is.</p>

        <p>The <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block is the page's setup: ordinary imports, ordinary
        TypeScript, run once for this instance of the page. Everything after it is the markup, and
        the file shares one scope — the template resolves names against its two scripts and
        nothing else.</p>

        <p><code>{'import { reactive } from \'@abide/abide\''}</code> is a real import. Nothing in a
        <code>.abide</code> file is ambient, so a name in the markup is a name you can follow back
        to where it came from.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`count` is read by name, and written by name">
        <p>Inside a <code>.abide</code> file, a reactive value is read and written under its own
        name.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>It means</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{count}'}</code> in the markup</td><td>read the current value, and follow it from here on</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>count += 1</code> in a handler</td><td>write, and wake everything that read it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>count.busy()</code></td><td>a probe — the <code>Reactive</code> API, on a member call</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Naming <code>count</code> in an expression <strong>reads</strong> it; the explicit
        <code>count()</code> and <code>count.set(v)</code> keep compiling everywhere the sugar does,
        because the sugar is over those forms rather than instead of them. A <code>.ts</code> file —
        which has no template around it — writes them out:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/ui/counters.ts" code={`import { reactive } from '@abide/abide'

export const count = reactive(0)
count.set(count() + 1)`} />

        <p>Reading <code>count</code> in the markup is also the whole subscription. There is nothing
        to register and no dependency array to keep honest: the heading reads it, so the heading
        follows it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/read-and-write-a-value-by-name')}>Values by name</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/respond-to-a-click')}>Events</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page reaches your database from its own server block">
        <p>The same page reaches your database through a handler its own
        <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> declares, and reads it like any other value.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/[id]/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<script server>
import { GET } from '@abide/abide/server'
import { database } from '#src/server/database'

export const getInvoice = GET(
    ({ id }: { id: string }) => database.invoice.find(id),
    { description: 'One invoice, by id.' },
)
</script>

<script>
import { reactive, route } from '@abide/abide'

const invoice = reactive(() => getInvoice({ id: route.params.id }))
</script>

<h1>Invoice {invoice.number}</h1>
<p>{invoice.total} due {invoice.dueOn}</p>`} />

        <p>No <code>fetch</code>, no client module, and no second copy of the invoice type. The
        browser is never sent the server block — the build generates a client from the handler in
        its place, so the database import above was never in anything the page downloaded.</p>

        <p>Reading <code>invoice</code> starts the load, and the document goes out before the row
        lands: the two values are holes that fill when it does. That is the default, and it is why
        neither line above has a loading branch in it.</p>

        <p>A handler several pages share goes in a file of its own: any <code>.abide</code> can
        hold a <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code>, it answers at its directory's address, and a page
        imports it like any other module.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The `<script>` setup runs on both sides">
        <p>The cost of one file per page is that its setup is not browser-only. abide renders the
        page on the server and the client runs the same setup again, so a
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block that reaches for <code>document</code> at the top level
        fails on the first render rather than on the second.</p>

        <p>Work that belongs to a real browser goes where there is one: an event handler, or a
        component's own load hook.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/start/how-a-page-becomes-html')}>Request to paint</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/run-code-when-a-component-loads')}>Scripts</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`abide dev` keeps the app up while you edit">
        <p><code>abide scaffold my-app</code> writes a project with this page already in it, and
        <code>abide dev</code> keeps the app up while you edit — one process, restarted on every
        change, with the browser reloading behind it.</p>

        <Code code={`abide scaffold my-app
abide dev`} />

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/start-a-new-app')}>Scaffold</a> ·
        <a href={url('/ship/run-the-app-while-you-work')}>Dev server</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/start/how-a-page-becomes-html')}>Request to paint</a> — what happened
            between the request and the pixels</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a> — the
            handler side, properly</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates')}>Templates</a> — conditionals, lists, components and
            forms</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
